=== Live end-to-end exploit reproduction (ad hoc verification script, NOT part of the committed suite — run once, standalone, to confirm the C3 gap above is genuinely exploitable via HTTP login and not merely a DB-column artifact) ===
Setup: operator row created with isAdmin=true (simulating ADR F5 "already isAdmin=true from a previous boot"), then apply_admin_rotation() run with AETHER_ADMIN_EMAIL=<that operator's email>, AETHER_ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH=bcrypt('admin123'), AETHER_ENV=production.
Result:
  apply_admin_rotation raised: AdminCredentialSecurityError BLOCKER-001: refusing to grant admin privilege to 'owner-79693da8@aether.io' ...
  LOGIN STATUS: 200
  LOGIN BODY: {"access_token":"eyJ...","token_type":"bearer","userId":"c6e975b89eef0a35f4f64780e","email":"owner-79693da8@aether.io"}
  ME STATUS: 200 BODY: {"id":"c6e975b89eef0a35f4f64780e","email":"owner-79693da8@aether.io","name":"","targetRole":"","location":"","isAdmin":true}
  ADMIN/USERS STATUS: 200
Interpretation: POST /auth/login with the OPERATOR'S EMAIL (not the reserved
"admin" username — commit 7f82105's compensating weak_operator_credential_refused
login guard only checks the literal "admin" identifier, per its own documented
SCOPE comment) and the literal password "admin123" succeeds with isAdmin:true,
and GET /admin/users (full user PII listing) returns 200. This is BLOCKER-001's
original exploit, reproduced against commit 7f82105, via the email vector the
ADR's own severity-escalation section (§1.2) specifically anticipated
("the attacker simply substitutes the email address printed in the public
repo").
